{
  "dom_id": "step-q183359",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "If $A$ is true and $B$ is true, then $¬A⇔B$ is{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $P⇔Q$ is true if $P⇒Q$ **and** $Q⇒P.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $P⇔Q$ is true if $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nSo, when $A=\\text{T}$ and $B=\\text{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬A⇔B) & =(¬\\text{T}⇔\\text{T}) \\\\  & =(\\text{F}⇔\\text{T}) \\\\  & =\\text{F}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe complete truth table is shown below:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A\\,$ | $\\,¬A⇔B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
